post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Wacom CTL-4100 userspace driver installed.

The udev rule grants this session access via uaccess, so normally no extra
group membership is needed.

Enable the daemon:
  sudo modprobe uinput
  systemctl --user enable --now wacomd

wacomd keeps the in-kernel `wacom` driver bound but grabs its evdev nodes
exclusively, so pen events are not delivered twice. Plug the tablet after
installation.

If wacomd reports permission denied on /dev/uinput or /dev/hidraw*:
  sudo usermod -aG input "$USER"
then log out and back in.
EOF
  udevadm control --reload-rules 2>/dev/null || true
  udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=hid 2>/dev/null || true
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}

pre_remove() {
  systemctl --user disable --now wacomd 2>/dev/null || true
}
